\title[Which Terms Carry the Exponent?]{Which Terms Carry the Exponent?\\
The Delete-a-Term Screen, the Redundant Smoothness Leg,\\
and the Load-Bearing Baby-Step Reuse in Harvey's $N^{1/5}$ Balance}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{October 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Harvey's deterministic factoring algorithm runs in time $N^{1/5+o(1)}$. With logarithmic factors dropped, its running time is the maximum of three power-of-$N$ costs, and the exponent $1/5$ is the value of the linear minimax $\min_{a,b}\max\{a,\,b,\,\tfrac12-\tfrac a2-b\}$, where $r=N^a$ is the size of a pair family and $m=N^b$ the length of a baby-step list. A fourth, smoothness-sieve term $(N/r)^{1/4}$ appears in a refined accounting and is large in practice. We study which terms of such a balance actually carry the exponent. We prove a general \emph{delete-a-term screen}: a term is worth optimising if and only if deleting it (or making it free) changes the minimax value. We show that the smoothness leg is exponent-redundant. With a leg $(N/r)^\theta$ the value is exactly $\max\{1/5,\theta/(1+\theta)\}$, and the actual leg $\theta=1/4$ sits on the breakpoint: it is active at the optimum but deletable. Exactly three of the four terms are load-bearing. The baby-step reuse is essential: without it the value is Lehman's $1/3$, and a single-product-tree ``collapse'' coupling has value exactly $1/4$, a penalty of exactly $N^{1/20}$, consistent with a reported experimental slope of $0.046$--$0.052$. We price the untouched lever: an $m^{1-\varepsilon}$ baby-step table gives exactly $(1-\varepsilon)/(5-3\varepsilon)$. Separately, we prove the general-exponent reveal identity $R(E)=d_p(q-1)+d_q(p-1)-2d_pd_q$ for gcd-based factor revelation, and show that its maximal-density case is factored by one integer square root. The exponent $1/5$ is not improved and remains an upper bound.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The deterministic factoring exponent}
A \emph{deterministic} factoring algorithm uses neither randomness nor unproved hypotheses. The best known running times for such algorithms have the form $N^{e+o(1)}$. Trial division gives $e=1/2$. Lehman's method gives $e=1/3$. The Pollard--Strassen method, which evaluates a product of consecutive integers with fast polynomial multiplication and multipoint evaluation, gives $e=1/4$. Harvey lowered the exponent to $e=1/5$, combining a Lehman-style family of candidate pairs with a baby-step giant-step search.

These are upper bounds. No superpolynomial lower bound for factoring is known in any general model. Results ruling out proposed improvements are not lower bounds on factoring. They describe the structure of a particular family of algorithms, so that effort is not spent on terms that cannot move the exponent.

\subsection{The exponent model}
Write $r=N^a$ and $m=N^b$ and drop all polylogarithmic factors. Harvey's algorithm then has three costs:
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{llll}
\toprule
term & meaning & cost & exponent\\
\midrule
pair family & candidate pairs enumerated & $r$ & $a$\\
baby-step list & stored powers $\alpha^0,\dots,\alpha^{m-1}$ & $m$ & $b$\\
interior & remaining giant-step search & $N^{1/2}/(r^{1/2}m)$ & $\tfrac12-\tfrac a2-b$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
The total exponent is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:cost}
c(a,b)=\max\Big\{a,\ b,\ \tfrac12-\tfrac a2-b\Big\}.
\end{equation}
A refined accounting adds a smoothness-sieve term $M^{1/2}=(N/r)^{1/4}$, of exponent $\tfrac14(1-a)$, which we call the \emph{M-leg}. At $N\approx2^{100}$, once polylogarithmic weights are included, it was estimated to take about one quarter of the budget. An earlier analysis listed it as one of three co-leading terms.

\subsection{Questions and answers}
\begin{enumerate}[label=\textbf{Q\arabic*.}]
\item Is the M-leg a genuine constraint on the exponent? \emph{No} (Theorem~\ref{thm:redundant}).
\item Is the reuse of the baby-step list essential, or slack? \emph{Essential}, by exactly $N^{1/20}$ against the natural alternative (Section~\ref{sec:reuse}).
\item Is there a cheap, general test for such questions? \emph{Yes}, the delete-a-term screen (Theorem~\ref{thm:screen}).
\end{enumerate}
Section~\ref{sec:reveal} adds an independent counting result about gcd-based factor revelation for arbitrary exponents.

\section{Minimax values and the delete-a-term screen}

\begin{definition}
Let $X$ be a set and $F:X\to\Q$. A number $c$ is a \emph{minimax lower bound} for $F$ if $c\le F(x)$ for all $x\in X$. A number $v$ is the \emph{minimax value} of $F$ if it is a minimax lower bound and $F(x_0)=v$ for some $x_0\in X$.
\end{definition}
The value is unique when it exists. If $v,w$ are values attained at $x,y$, then $v\le F(y)=w\le F(x)=v$. In our applications $F=\max_iT_i$ is a maximum of affine \emph{terms}, so computing the value is a small linear program.

\begin{theorem}[Delete-a-term screen]\label{thm:screen}
Let $F':X\to\Q$ be the maximum of all terms but one, and let $c\in\Q$. Then $c$ is a minimax lower bound for $\max\{F',g'\}$ for \emph{every} $g':X\to\Q$ if and only if $c$ is a minimax lower bound for $F'$. Equivalently, some modification $g'$ achieves $\max\{F'(x),g'(x)\}<v$ at some $x$ if and only if $F'(x)<v$ at some $x$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $c\le F'$, then $c\le F'\le\max\{F',g'\}$. Conversely, take $g'=F'$, so that $\max\{F',F'\}=F'$.
\end{proof}

\noindent\textbf{Screening rule.} \emph{In a minimax cost $\max\{T_1,\dots,T_k\}$, a term is worth optimising if and only if deleting it (equivalently, making it free) moves the minimax value. A term whose deletion does not move the value is worthless to optimise, whatever its size.}

\begin{lemma}[Inert term preserves the value]\label{lem:inert}
If $v$ is the minimax value of $F'$, attained at $x_0$, and $g(x_0)\le v$, then $\max\{F',g\}$ has minimax value exactly $v$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$v\le F'\le\max\{F',g\}$ everywhere, and $\max\{F'(x_0),g(x_0)\}=v$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Monotonicity]
If $F'$ has value $v$ and $\max\{F',g\}$ has value $w$, then $v\le w$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
At a point $x$ attaining $w$, we have $v\le F'(x)\le w$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Screening is relative]\label{rem:relative}
Redundancy depends on the set of terms a term is compared against, and it is not preserved when other terms are deleted. One should screen one term against a fixed set, prune it if inert, and screen again. Remark~\ref{rem:instance} gives an example.
\end{remark}

\begin{proposition}[Harvey balance]\label{prop:harvey}
The function $c$ in \eqref{eq:cost}, over $(a,b)\in\Q^2$, has minimax value exactly $1/5$, attained at $(1/5,1/5)$. Moreover, $c(a,b)\le1/5$ implies $a=b=1/5$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
With weights $\tfrac15,\tfrac25,\tfrac25$ we get $\tfrac15a+\tfrac25b+\tfrac25(\tfrac12-\tfrac a2-b)=\tfrac15$. A convex combination is at most the maximum, so $c\ge1/5$. At $a=b=\tfrac15$ all three terms equal $\tfrac15$. If all terms are $\le\tfrac15$, then $\tfrac a2+b\ge\tfrac3{10}$ with $a,b\le\tfrac15$, which forces $a=b=\tfrac15$.
\end{proof}

\section{The M-leg is exponent-redundant}
For $\theta\ge0$ put $c_\theta(a,b)=\max\{c(a,b),\ \theta(1-a)\}$. The M-leg is $\theta=1/4$. A completely free leg is $\theta=0$.

\begin{theorem}[Redundancy of the M-leg]\label{thm:redundant}
For every $\theta\le1/4$, the minimax value of $c_\theta$ is exactly $1/5$. This includes the actual leg $\theta=1/4$ and the free leg $\theta=0$, and it coincides with the value when the leg is deleted.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:inert} with $x_0=(1/5,1/5)$. We need $\tfrac45\theta\le\tfrac15$, i.e.\ $\theta\le\tfrac14$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Value above the breakpoint]\label{thm:above}
For $\theta\ge1/4$ the minimax value of $c_\theta$ is exactly $\theta/(1+\theta)$, attained at $a=\theta/(1+\theta)$, $b=(1-a)/4$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $v=c_\theta(a,b)$, then $a\le v$ and $\theta(1-a)\le v$. Hence $\theta\le(1+\theta)v$. At the displayed point, $\theta(1-a)=a$, the interior equals $\tfrac{1-a}4=b$, and $b\le a$ is equivalent to $\theta\ge\tfrac14$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Value function]
For all $\theta\ge0$, $V(\theta)=\max\{\tfrac15,\ \tfrac{\theta}{1+\theta}\}$. It is flat on $[0,\tfrac14]$ and strictly increasing afterwards.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Strict breakpoint]
If $\theta>1/4$, then $c_\theta(a,b)>1/5$ for every $(a,b)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $c_\theta(a,b)\le\tfrac15$, then $c(a,b)\le\tfrac15$, so $a=\tfrac15$ by Proposition~\ref{prop:harvey}. But then $\theta(1-a)=\tfrac45\theta>\tfrac15$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The real leg is co-tight]
If $c(a,b)=1/5$, then $\tfrac14(1-a)=\tfrac15$. The actual M-leg is active at the unique optimum, yet deleting it does not change the value.
\end{theorem}

\section{Exactly three terms are load-bearing}
Restrict to the box $0\le a,b\le1$, i.e.\ $1\le r,m\le N$.

\begin{theorem}[Load-bearing classification]\label{thm:classify}
On the box:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
\item without the interior, $\max\{a,b\}$ has value $0$ (at $(0,0)$);
\item without the pair term, $\max\{b,\tfrac12-\tfrac a2-b\}$ has value $0$ (at $(1,0)$);
\item without the baby-step term, $\max\{a,\tfrac12-\tfrac a2-b\}$ has value $0$ (at $(0,\tfrac12)$);
\item for every replacement $g'$ of a fourth term, $\max\{c,g'\}\ge\tfrac15$.
\end{enumerate}
The three-term and four-term ($\theta=\tfrac14$) costs both have value $\tfrac15$ on the box.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
All terms are non-negative on the box, and each displayed point makes the remaining terms vanish. Item (4) is Theorem~\ref{thm:screen} applied to Proposition~\ref{prop:harvey}.
\end{proof}

So exactly three of the four terms carry the exponent. The M-leg is a co-leader only by polylogarithmic weight, not by exponent.

\begin{remark}\label{rem:instance}
If the M-leg is kept, deleting the interior leaves value $\tfrac15$ ($\max\{a,b,\tfrac14(1-a)\}$ at $a=\tfrac15$, $b=0$), and so does deleting $m$ ($a=\tfrac15$, $b=1$). In both cases the M-leg becomes the binding term. The classification is the outcome of a two-stage screen: prune the inert M-leg, then screen the core.
\end{remark}

\section{The baby-step reuse is load-bearing}\label{sec:reuse}

\begin{theorem}[No split is Lehman]
If $b=0$ (i.e.\ $m=1$), then $c(a,0)\ge\tfrac13$ for all $a$, with equality at $a=\tfrac13$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$c(a,0)\ge\max\{a,\tfrac12-\tfrac a2\}$. The first term is $\ge\tfrac13$ when $a\ge\tfrac13$, the second otherwise, and both equal $\tfrac13$ at $a=\tfrac13$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The split must be balanced]
If $b\ne\tfrac15$, then $c(a,b)>\tfrac15$ for every $a$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Proposition~\ref{prop:harvey}: equality forces $b=\tfrac15$.
\end{proof}

\subsection*{The collapse coupling}
A single Strassen-type product tree sweeping the pair grid and the interior together handles a search space of size $r\cdot N^{1/2}/r^{1/2}$ at square-root cost, i.e.\ exponent $\tfrac14+\tfrac a4$. With the pair enumeration this gives $c_{\mathrm{col}}(a)=\max\{a,\tfrac14+\tfrac a4\}$ for $a\ge0$.

\begin{theorem}[Collapse value and penalty]
Over $a\ge0$, $c_{\mathrm{col}}$ has minimax value exactly $\tfrac14$, attained at $a=0$. The difference from the value of $c$ is exactly $\tfrac14-\tfrac15=\tfrac1{20}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$c_{\mathrm{col}}(a)\ge\tfrac14+\tfrac a4\ge\tfrac14$, with equality at $a=0$. Then use Proposition~\ref{prop:harvey}.
\end{proof}

\subsection*{Experimental confirmation}
The collapse coupling was implemented as a complete factoring pipeline, with genuine number-theoretic-transform product trees and remainder-tree multipoint evaluation, and checked against ground-truth factorisations. We report the outcome as experimental data. The mechanism is a correct factoring algorithm. Its dominant input-size exponent is $0.2500$, not $\le0.20$. Its fitted penalty slope is $0.046$--$0.052$, against $1/20=0.0500$. The fit also refutes a table of large constants that had been proposed in its support. The $m$-reuse is load-bearing, not slack.

\section{Pricing the remaining lever}

\begin{theorem}[Baby-step discount]
For $0\le\varepsilon<1$, the cost $\max\{a,(1-\varepsilon)b,\tfrac12-\tfrac a2-b\}$ has minimax value exactly $\dfrac{1-\varepsilon}{5-3\varepsilon}$, attained at $a=\frac{1-\varepsilon}{5-3\varepsilon}$, $b=\frac1{5-3\varepsilon}$. It is $<\tfrac15$ for $\varepsilon>0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$(1-\varepsilon)a+2(1-\varepsilon)b+2(1-\varepsilon)(\tfrac12-\tfrac a2-b)=1-\varepsilon$, with weights summing to $5-3\varepsilon$, so $(5-3\varepsilon)v\ge1-\varepsilon$. All terms are equal at the displayed point. Strictness holds because $5(1-\varepsilon)<5-3\varepsilon$ if and only if $\varepsilon>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Interior gain]
$\min_{a,b}\max\{a,b,\tfrac12-\delta-\tfrac a2-b\}=\dfrac{1-2\delta}5$, attained at $a=b=\frac{1-2\delta}5$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[A misstated target]
The proposed target $N^{(1/2-\delta)/5}$ is off by a factor of two: $(1-2\delta)/5=2\cdot\frac{1/2-\delta}5$. At $\delta=0$ it would claim $1/10$, below the model's floor $1/5$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Exchange rate]
For $\varepsilon<1$, $\dfrac{1-2\varepsilon/(5-3\varepsilon)}5=\dfrac{1-\varepsilon}{5-3\varepsilon}$. A baby-step discount $\varepsilon$ is worth an interior gain $\delta=\varepsilon/(5-3\varepsilon)$.
\end{theorem}
For instance, $\varepsilon=\tfrac14$ gives $3/17$ (equivalently $\delta=1/17$), and $\varepsilon=\tfrac12$ gives $1/7$.

\section{The general-exponent reveal identity}\label{sec:reveal}
Let $N=pq$ with distinct odd primes $p,q$, and identify $(\Z/N)^\times\cong(\Z/p)^\times\times(\Z/q)^\times$. For a unit $a\leftrightarrow(v_1,v_2)$, $\gcd(a^E-1,N)$ is a proper factor exactly when one of $v_1^E=1$, $v_2^E=1$ holds and the other fails.

\begin{definition}
$d_p=\gcd(E,p-1)$, $d_q=\gcd(E,q-1)$, and $R(E)$ is the number of units for which exactly one of $v_1^E=1$, $v_2^E=1$ holds.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}
In the cyclic group $(\Z/p)^\times$, the equation $x^E=1$ has exactly $\gcd(E,p-1)$ solutions.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
For a generator $h$, $h^j$ is a solution if and only if $\frac{p-1}{\gcd(E,p-1)}\mid j$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Component counts]
For every $E$: $\#\{v_1^E=1\}=d_p(q-1)$; $\#\{v_1^E=1,v_2^E=1\}=d_pd_q$; $\#\{v_1^E=1,v_2^E\ne1\}=d_p(q-1)-d_pd_q$; and $\#\{v_2^E=1,v_1^E\ne1\}=d_q(p-1)-d_pd_q$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[General-exponent reveal identity]
For every exponent $E$,
\[R(E)=d_p(q-1)+d_q(p-1)-2d_pd_q.\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Add the last two component counts. The subtractions are valid because $d_p\le p-1$ and $d_q\le q-1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Recovery of the Euler-gap law]
At $E=N-1$, both gcds equal $g=\gcd(p-1,q-1)$, and $R(N-1)=g(q-1)+g(p-1)-2g^2$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proposition}[Four-cell partition]
With $P=p-1$, $Q=q-1$, $x=d_p\le P$, $y=d_q\le Q$:
\[xy+(xQ+yP-2xy)+(P-x)(Q-y)=PQ.\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Write $P=x+s$, $Q=y+t$. The middle term is $xt+ys$, and $xy+xt+ys+st=PQ$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Square discriminant]
For integers $p,k,s$ with $q=k(p-1)+s$ and $N=pq$: $kp^2+(s-k)p-N=0$ and $(s-k)^2+4kN=(2kp+s-k)^2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[One integer square root]
If $q=k(p-1)+1$ with $k,p\ge1$, then $p=\big(\lfloor\sqrt{(k-1)^2+4kN}\rfloor+(k-1)\big)/(2k)$. If $q=k(p-1)-1$ with $k\ge1$, $p\ge2$, then $p=\big(\lfloor\sqrt{(k+1)^2+4kN}\rfloor+(k+1)\big)/(2k)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[The maximal-gap extreme]
Let $p<q$ be primes with $(p-1)\mid(q-1)$, and $q=k(p-1)+1$. Then $R(N-1)=(p-1)(q-p)$, so the reveal density is $1-1/k$, and $p$ is recovered from $N$ and $k$ by a single integer square root.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
With $g=p-1$: $(p-1)(q-1)+(p-1)^2-2(p-1)^2=(p-1)(q-p)$. The second claim is the previous corollary.
\end{proof}
The high-density end of the gcd-reveal family is exactly its algebraically solvable end.

\section{Algorithms and examples}
\begin{algorithm}[Exact two-parameter minimax]
Form the lines given by the box edges and $T_i=T_j$. Intersect every pair of non-parallel lines, keep the points in the box, and return the point minimising $\max_iT_i$. The cost is $O(k^4)$ in exact rationals.
\end{algorithm}
\begin{algorithm}[Delete-a-term screen]
Compute the value $v$. For each term $T$, if deleting $T$ leaves value $v$, remove $T$ and restart. The terms that remain are load-bearing.
\end{algorithm}
\begin{algorithm}[Extreme factoring]
For $k\le K$ and $s=\pm1$: if $D=(s-k)^2+4kN$ is a perfect square $\rho^2$ and $p=(\rho-(s-k))/(2k)$ is a proper divisor of $N$, output $p$.
\end{algorithm}
\noindent\emph{Examples.} $N=2035153=1009\cdot2017$ (with $k=2$, density $\tfrac12$). $N=10374439=1019\cdot10181$ ($k=10$, density $\tfrac9{10}$). $N=1091501=523\cdot2087$ ($s=-1$, $k=4$). For $p=13$, $q=37$, a direct count confirms $R(36)=0$ and $R(480)=288$.

\section{Discussion and future directions}
The exponent $1/5$ is not improved, and it remains an upper bound. Sections 3--6 concern the exponent model with polylogarithmic factors dropped. After this work, the exponent is known to be carried by the interior, the pair family and the baby-step list. The M-leg is inert, and the $m$-reuse is worth exactly $N^{1/20}$.
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
\item \emph{Sub-linear baby-step tables.} Conjecture: in the generic-group model, no table of $\alpha^0,\dots,\alpha^{m-1}$ with polylogarithmic membership queries can be built in $m^{1-\varepsilon}$ operations.
\item \emph{Breakpoint rigidity.} For $\max\{a,b,\gamma-\tfrac a2-b\}$ with leg $\theta(1-a)$, the breakpoint should be $\theta^*=(2\gamma/5)/(1-2\gamma/5)$, which is $\tfrac14$ at $\gamma=\tfrac12$.
\item \emph{Reveal-density maximisers are algebraic.} For fixed $k$, integers $N=pq$ with $R(N-1)/\varphi(N)\ge1-1/k$ should be factorable by at most $k$ integer square roots.
\item \emph{Optimal reveal exponents.} One expects $\max_ER(E)/\varphi(N)=1-(\tfrac1{p-1}+\tfrac1{q-1})+O(1/pq)$.
\end{enumerate}

